Title:
WindowsSentinel: WDAC Deployment & Hardening Tool

Type:
GitHub Tool

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
WindowsSentinel is a standalone Windows batch script that builds and deploys Windows Defender Application Control (WDAC) policies on Windows 10/11 and Server 2016+ to enforce application allowlisting at the kernel Code Integrity layer. It aims to close the gap between audit-only hardening checkers (e.g., tools that identify misconfigurations) and actual remediation by scanning installed software, generating publisher/path allow rules, and deploying a compiled WDAC policy. The tool also supports explicit deny rules for a selectable set of 31 commonly abused LOLBAS binaries, with deny rules taking precedence even over Microsoft-signed trust. An optional “/harden” mode adds insider-threat style controls via NTFS ACL restrictions on denied binaries, machine-wide registry/GPO-style settings (e.g., disabling cmd/registry tools for standard users), and WDAC path deny rules for user-writable directories. It includes an audit-to-enforce workflow with reporting based on CodeIntegrity event logs (3076/3077) and provides rollback via a generated PowerShell script or WinRE deletion of active CIP policies. Useful primarily for defenders (SOC analysts, sysadmins, blue teams) and for red teams as a reference target environment for WDAC/CI bypass research and operational constraints.

Technical Focus:
- Windows Defender Application Control (WDAC) policy generation and deployment
- Windows Code Integrity (ci.dll) enforcement, Audit vs Enforce modes
- Publisher/path/deny rule design (including LOLBAS denylisting)
- CodeIntegrity event log monitoring (Event IDs 3076/3077)
- NTFS ACL-based execution/read restrictions for standard users
- Blocking execution from user-writable paths via WDAC path deny rules

Use Cases:
- Rapid WDAC rollout on endpoints/servers with guided audit → enforce workflow
- Denylisting high-risk LOLBAS executables to reduce living-off-the-land tradecraft
- Creating a hardened workstation baseline (including user-writable path execution blocks)
- Generating WDAC audit reports to tune allowlists before enforcement
- Incident response containment by quickly enforcing application control and restricting tooling

Keywords:
WDAC, Windows Defender Application Control, Code Integrity, ci.dll, CIPolicy, DefaultWindows_Enforced.xml, ConfigCI, citool.exe, Audit Mode, Enforce Mode, publisher rules, path rules, deny rules, LOLBAS, msbuild.exe, certutil.exe, powershell.exe, msiexec.exe, rundll32.exe, Microsoft-Windows-CodeIntegrity/Operational, Event ID 3076, Event ID 3077, NTFS ACL, WinRE, CIPolicies\Active, application allowlisting